= Fish — General Overview

_A category-level entry, sitting above the species-specific salmon (salmon.typ) and sardines (sardines.typ) entries — and parallel in role to the four-tea-categories framework treated in the tea entries. Fish is a category large enough to merit a synthesizing framework that organizes the_ classical pharmacological treatments _across the major Eurasian and indigenous traditions, the_ traditional preparation methods _(which are themselves saṃskāra-class transformations in the classical sense), and the_ modern safety and pharmacology considerations _(which apply across the species). The species-specific entries cover what is distinctive about salmon and sardines; this entry covers the cross-cutting framework._

== Scope and classification

Fish — *aquatic vertebrates with gills*, both freshwater and marine — comprise a vast diversity (~34,000 living species) of which a few hundred are commercially important food fishes. The category in classical pharmacology is universal: every Eurasian classical tradition has a *fish* (matsya / 魚 / ἰχθύς / piscis) general category, with subdivisions and species-specific drugs.

*Classification axes relevant to pharmacology and consumption:*

1. *Habitat:* freshwater (river, lake, pond, paddy) vs. *brackish* (estuarine) vs. *marine* (coastal vs. deep-water). Different habitats give different fatty acid profiles, contaminant exposures, and traditional uses.

2. *Trophic level:*
- *Low trophic level (planktivorous / herbivorous)* — small clupeoids (sardines, anchovies, sprats), filter feeders. *Low contaminant bioaccumulation; high omega-3 by mass.*
- *Mid trophic level* — many demersal fish (cod, sole, hake), salmonids (salmon, trout), modest predators.
- *High trophic level (apex predators)* — large tunas (bluefin, yellowfin, bigeye), swordfish, marlin, shark, king mackerel. *High contaminant bioaccumulation (mercury especially); to be limited in pregnancy and pediatric diets.*

3. *Fat content:*
- *Lean fish (under 5% fat):* cod, haddock, pollock, sole, flounder, sea bass, perch, pike, snapper, grouper, tilapia, catfish. Low omega-3 but high protein and easy digestion.
- *Oily fish (above 5% fat):* salmon, sardines, anchovies, mackerel, herring, trout, tuna (varies), eel. *High long-chain omega-3 (EPA, DHA), vitamin D, vitamin B12, vitamin A in some.* The principal cardiovascular and developmental-omega-3 source.

4. *Body size / longevity:* small short-lived (sardines, anchovies) — low contaminants. Large long-lived (tuna, swordfish, shark) — high contaminants. The relationship between body size + trophic level + lifespan and *mercury bioaccumulation* is the foundational modern safety axis.

5. *Origin:* wild vs. *farmed (aquaculture)*. Wild has lower antibiotic residue, generally lower PCBs (varies); farmed has higher omega-3 by mass (controlled feed), more consistent quality, sometimes higher PCB / dioxin (improving with regulation); reputable certifications (MSC for wild, ASC for farmed) provide some assurance.

== Cross-system classical pharmacology

=== Ayurvedic matsya

_Matsya_ (मत्स्य) is the general fish category in the Ayurvedic saṃhitās — Caraka Saṃhitā Sūtrasthāna 27 (annapānavidhi) treats fish in a chapter section, with sub-classifications.

*Sub-classifications:*
- *Nādī-matsya* (नादीमत्स्य) — riverine / freshwater fish; classically *heavier* and considered *more nourishing* (and more Pitta-aggravating).
- *Samudra-matsya* (समुद्रमत्स्य) — marine fish; classically *lighter*.
- *Saraḥ-matsya* (सरःमत्स्य) — lake / tank fish.

*General dravyaguna of matsya:*
- *Rasa:* Madhura (sweet).
- *Guṇa:* Guru (heavy), Snigdha (unctuous).
- *Vīrya:* Uṣṇa (warming) — for most fish; some freshwater species classed as śīta (cooling) in commentary.
- *Vipāka:* Madhura (sweet).
- *Doṣa:* Pacifies Vāta; aggravates Pitta and Kapha; *the strongly snigdha-uṣṇa profile makes fish appropriate for Vāta-vacuity nourishment but inappropriate for Pitta-inflammation*.
- *Karma:* _balya_ (strengthening), _bṛṃhaṇa_ (nourishing / anabolic), _vṛṣya_ (aphrodisiac, śukrala), _hṛdya_ (cardiotonic). Used in convalescence from Vāta-pattern depletion.

*Classical viruddha — the famous fish-milk rule:* *fish + milk together is the canonical saṃyoga viruddha of the Caraka tradition* — held to cause _rakta-duṣṭi_ (blood-pathology) and skin disease (_kuṣṭha_). The rule is universally cited; the modern toxicological basis is not established but the cross-cultural avoidance is striking. Other fish viruddhas: with milk; with curd in some preparations; with sour fruits in some texts.#footnote[Matsya in Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 27 (annapānavidhi, fish section); Suśruta Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 46; Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Māṃsa-varga_ (where fish is treated alongside other meats). Fish-milk viruddha: Caraka Sūtra 26 (viruddhāhāra). Translations: Sharma & Dash, Murthy.]

=== Classical Chinese pharmacology

The Chinese Bencao tradition treats numerous fish species as distinct drugs, each with its own property and indications. Tang to Ming pharmacopoeias include extensive fish entries.

*Representative classical fish drugs (selected):*
- *鯉魚 (lǐyú, carp)* — sweet, neutral; Spleen / Stomach; pregnancy and postpartum tonic; resolves edema; the canonical Chinese "blessing fish" of folk and dietetic tradition.
- *鯽魚 (jìyú, crucian carp)* — sweet, neutral; Spleen / Stomach; tonifies middle, resolves damp / edema.
- *鰻魚 (mányú, eel)* — sweet, slightly warm; Liver, Kidney; tonifies vacuity, strengthens bones, treats chronic skin disease; classical "longevity fish" of Chinese-Japanese folk medicine (rich in fat-soluble vitamins).
- *黃花魚 (huánghuāyú, large yellow croaker)*; *帶魚 (dàiyú, hairtail)*; *烏魚 (wūyú, snakehead)* — postpartum / convalescent recovery.
- *泥鰍 (níqiu, weather loach)* — Spleen-supportive; jaundice / damp-heat resolution.

*General Chinese reading*: fish in general are *tonifying, qi-and-blood supportive, warming the middle*; specific species have specific channels and uses. The Chinese tradition's species-specific fish-by-fish pharmacy is much more developed than the Ayurvedic generic matsya category.#footnote[Fish entries in Li Shizhen, _Bencao Gangmu_, 1578 — fish section is extensive; Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed., treats select fish drugs.]

=== Greco-Roman / Mediterranean classical

_Greek and Roman pharmacy treats fish extensively, both as food and as drugs._

- *Hippocratic Corpus* — fish in the dietetic and therapeutic register.
- *Dioscorides* (_De Materia Medica_, Book II) — multiple fish entries: fresh and salt-cured fish, oysters and shellfish, fish blood and bone, fish liver. The Mediterranean classical pharmacy treats fish as warming-cooling spectrum depending on species; oily fish (tunny, anchovy) as warming, white fish as more cooling.
- *Galen* classifies fish as warming and moistening in moderate degree; useful for the strong, less suitable for the weak in heavy doses.
- *Pliny* (_N.H._ IX) gives the Roman natural history of fish — extensive species treatment, fishing techniques, and pharmacy.
- *Apicius* preserves Roman fish cookery; the Roman *garum* (fermented fish sauce) industry was a foundational Mediterranean commerce — see sardines.typ.
- *Athenaeus's* _Deipnosophistae_ gives literary fish-cuisine.#footnote[Greco-Roman: Hippocrates _passim_; Dioscorides, _De Materia Medica_, Book II — fish entries; Galen, _On the Properties of Foodstuffs_; Pliny, _Naturalis Historia_ IX; Apicius; Athenaeus. The Curtis _Garum and Salsamenta_ for the garum economy.]

=== Indigenous Pacific Northwest

See salmon.typ for the substantive treatment of Pacific Northwest salmon traditions. The broader category includes *cedar-plank fish-cooking*, *smoked salmon and halibut*, *dried salmon (jerky)*, *oolichan (eulachon, candlefish)* — the small smelt with very high oil content, used as a *food, fuel, and trade commodity* in Pacific Northwest tradition; oolichan grease is a major traded substance. The Northwest coast fisheries' organized harvest of multiple species (salmon, halibut, oolichan, herring) supported population densities exceptional for hunter-gatherer-fisher societies.

=== Japanese fish tradition

Japan has the most-developed fish-cuisine and folk-medical tradition globally outside of the Chinese-pharmacy-influenced register.

- *Sashimi / sushi* — the raw-fish tradition; *technically demanding sourcing and handling* are integral to safety.
- *Yakizakana (grilled fish)* — the canonical Japanese breakfast and dinner preparation.
- *Nimono (simmered fish)* — soy / mirin / sake / ginger preparations.
- *Saba no shio-yaki* (salt-grilled mackerel), *iwashi no nimono*, etc.
- *Niboshi* (dried baby sardines for dashi) and *katsuobushi* (dried fermented bonito for dashi) — the foundational Japanese stock.
- *Fish as everyday food* with strong species-by-species pharmacological-folk register: tuna (maguro) for stamina, eel (unagi) for summer-heat-fatigue (the canonical _Doyo no Ushi no Hi_ summer-day eel tradition), bonito (katsuo) for vitality.
- *Fugu* (puffer fish) — the deliberately-controlled tetrodotoxin tradition; the high-art and high-risk preparation of poisonous fish.

=== Brazilian and South American tradition

- *Brazilian Atlantic*: sardinha verdadeira, robalo (sea bass), pescada, atum (tuna), tainha (mullet), peixe-galo, namorado, badejo (grouper).
- *Amazonian freshwater*: pirarucu (Arapaima gigas — one of the largest freshwater fish; iconic Amazonian food), tambaqui, tucunaré, pacu — the foundational Amazonian fish economy; the *moqueca de peixe* (fish stew with palm oil, coconut milk, peppers, coriander) of Bahia is a regional emblem.
- *Caribbean and Andean*: ceviche tradition (Peru), bacalao (Atlantic cod salt-cured, foundational to Portuguese and Brazilian _bacalhau_ tradition).
- *Brazilian moqueca* and the regional fish-and-palm-oil preparations are foundational coastal-Brazilian cuisine.

=== Religious dietary law overlays

- *Jewish kashrut* — only fish with *fins and scales* are kosher (Leviticus 11:9–12; Deuteronomy 14:9–10). Shellfish, crustaceans, octopus, squid, eels (no scales), and many others are not kosher. The fins-and-scales rule has been the canonical Jewish fish boundary for ~3000 years.
- *Islamic halal* — most schools (Sunni majority) consider all fish halal; Shia and some others restrict to fish with scales (similar to kashrut); shrimp and other shellfish vary by school.
- *Catholic Christianity* — historical practice of *Friday fast-from-meat* (substituted with fish, which was traditionally exempt); the medieval European fish economy was substantially shaped by Catholic Friday and Lent fasting requirements, supporting the Atlantic cod-and-herring trade.
- *Buddhist and Jain traditions* — most strict vegetarian schools exclude fish; some Tibetan Buddhist contexts permit fish; *Japanese Buddhism historically permitted fish more than land-meat* (the relative-purity ranking of marine vs. land animals in Japanese-Buddhist dietary practice).
- *Hindu* — varies regionally; *Bengali, Kerala, and coastal-Indian Hindu communities historically eat fish*, while many North Indian Brahmin communities are strictly vegetarian and exclude fish.#footnote[Religious dietary law overlays: standard religious-studies literature; Leviticus 11; Catholic medieval ecclesiastical fasting tradition; Buddhist dietary literature.]

=== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani) — _samak_

_Fish are extensively treated in classical Tibb under_ samak (سمك) _— a general category with sub-classifications by habitat, body, and method of preservation. See_ tibb-al-arabi.typ _for the framework._

*General mizāj of fresh fish (Avicenna):* *Cold and Moist in the 1st–2nd degree* — with substantial variation. Saltwater fish slightly warmer-and-drier than freshwater; oily fish (sardine, anchovy, mackerel) somewhat warmer than lean white fish; salt-cured and fermented fish *Hot and Dry* in the 2nd–3rd degree (the cure transforms the mizāj). The Tibb classification *diverges from the Ayurvedic uṣṇa vīrya (warming)* of fresh fish — the medieval Arabic-Persian reading is closer to cooling-moistening, reflecting the Mediterranean climatic register.

*Sub-classifications in Tibb literature:*
- *Samak al-baḥr* (saltwater fish) — moderate-Cold-Moist; the canonical Mediterranean reference.
- *Samak al-nahr* (riverine / freshwater fish) — Cold-Moist; lighter and easier to digest in the Tibb framework, *the opposite of the Ayurvedic reading* where freshwater fish is heavier.
- *Samak ṭarī* (fresh fish) — preferred; rapid spoilage means timing-of-consumption is critical (the canonical Tibb rule: *fish must be eaten the same day caught* — the rule appears in Avicenna and is universal in Tibb).
- *Samak mumallaḥ / mumallaḥ ṭurshī* (salt-cured) — Hot-Dry, suitable for cold-constitution appetite-loss but inflammatory.
- *Murrī / ṣīr / garum equivalents* (fermented fish sauce) — Hot-Dry, condimental.

*Af'āl:* *Mughadhdhī* (nourishing); *muḥarrik al-bāh* (aphrodisiac — fish is the canonical Tibb aphrodisiac-protein, paralleling the Ayurvedic _vṛṣya_); *muḥsin al-jasad*; *muḥarrik al-akhlāṭ* (humor-stirring — the key caution: fish stirs humors and can release latent disease in the susceptible).

*Istemālāt:* sexual debility; convalescence (fresh white fish); cold-constitution appetite-loss (salt-cured); general nourishment.

*Mawāne' (canonical Tibb fish-incompatibilities):*
- *Fish + milk* — *the canonical Tibb-Ayurvedic convergent saṃyoga viruddha*. Avicenna explicitly forbids fish + milk in the *Qānūn*; the rule converges precisely with Caraka's _matsya-kṣīra viruddha_. Modern toxicological basis remains unproven but the cross-system convergence is one of the cleanest classical incompatibilities.
- *Fish + sour fruits* — heavy combinations are cautioned in some Tibb sources.
- *Spoiled fish* — emphasized strongly throughout Tibb literature; Avicenna warns of putrefaction-induced toxicity. The classical concern aligns with the modern scombroid-histamine pharmacology.

*Muṣliḥ:* vinegar; lemon; coriander; cumin; olive oil; the Mediterranean *escabeche / scapace / pickled-fish* tradition observes the muṣliḥ pairings precisely.

*Tibb formulas and culinary traditions:* the medieval Arab-Andalusian cookery (al-Baghdādī's *Kitāb al-Ṭabīkh*, 13th c.) records fish preparations with the muṣliḥ pairings; the Tibb-pharmacy register treats fish principally as a dietetic food rather than as a compound-formula ingredient. Specific medical fish-preparations: *Mā' al-samak* (fish broth for convalescent debility); *Murrī*-based digestive preparations.

*Source attestation:* Ibn Sīnā, *al-Qānūn* II, _samak_ entry and species sub-entries; Ibn al-Bayṭār, *al-Jāmiʿ*; al-Rāzī, *al-Ḥāwī*; *Khazāyin al-Adwiya*; al-Baghdādī, *Kitāb al-Ṭabīkh*.#footnote[Tibb samak: extensive classical Avicennan treatment; the fish-milk saṃyoga viruddha is a clean cross-system convergence with Ayurveda.]

== Traditional preparation methods — saṃskāra-class transformations

The pre-modern preservation and preparation methods are themselves a *saṃskāra*-class system — the substance is *transformed* by the method, with different pharmacological properties from the fresh original. The classical traditions are alert to this; the modern pharmacology vindicates many of the classical distinctions.

=== 1. Fresh

- *Cooked fresh fish* — the dominant register globally; pharmacology of the fresh fish, modulated by cooking method (grill / poach / steam / fry / bake).
- *Raw fresh fish (sashimi, ceviche, tartare, kinilaw)* — the high-end and high-risk register; requires sourcing for parasite-free supply and freezing for anisakis inactivation (modern regulatory practice). Ceviche (citrus cure) does not reliably kill parasites despite popular belief.

=== 2. Cold-smoked

- *Smoked salmon (lox), smoked trout, smoked mackerel* — cured at low temperature (under ~30 °C) with smoke; the *protein remains essentially raw* but is cured by salt and the smoke-derived phenolics; *parasites are not reliably killed* by cold-smoking alone (require prior freezing).
- *Lapsang-style fish smoking in northern European tradition*.

=== 3. Hot-smoked

- *Hot-smoked salmon, smoked mackerel, smoked herring* — cured at higher temperature (60–80 °C); the protein is fully cooked.
- Tradition: Scottish smoked salmon, Polish smoked herring, Russian smoked fish, Pacific Northwest smoked salmon.

=== 4. Salt-cured (dried, semi-dried, wet-brined)

- *Bacalhau / bacalao / klippfisch — salt-cured Atlantic cod* — *the foundational Portuguese / Brazilian / Spanish / Italian-Adriatic / Norwegian fish*; salted and air-dried; reconstituted by long soaking; the basis of Portuguese-Brazilian _bacalhau à brás_, _bacalhau à Gomes de Sá_, Norwegian _lutefisk_, Italian _baccalà_, and other dishes. Historical importance: Atlantic cod salt-curing supported the Catholic Friday fast economy and was a foundational European-American Atlantic commerce.
- *Sardines, anchovies salt-cured* — Mediterranean.
- *Salted dried small fish (niboshi, dashima-anchovies)* — Japanese, Korean.
- *Brazilian piracuí* (smoke-dried pirarucu).

=== 5. Pickled / marinated

- *Rollmops* (German pickled herring), *escabeche* (Spanish-Portuguese-Latin American pickled fish), *Polish-Russian śledzie* — vinegar-and-spice marinated fish.

=== 6. Fermented — the most pharmacologically distinctive

- *Garum / liquamen* — the foundational Roman fermented fish sauce; salt-fermented small fish; the major Roman trade commodity.
- *Vietnamese nước mắm*, *Thai nam pla*, *Filipino patis*, *Italian colatura di alici (Cetara)* — the surviving global fish-sauce tradition; salt-fermentation by enzymes from the fish itself plus *Tetragenococcus* / *Bacillus* and other halotolerant microbes. The *substantial free-amino-acid (especially glutamate)* content is the basis of the umami flavor and is one of the world's most-distributed umami foods.
- *Surströmming* (Swedish lightly-fermented Baltic herring) — distinctive smell; foundational Northern European tradition.
- *Hákarl* (Icelandic fermented shark) — *Greenland shark, which is toxic raw* due to high trimethylamine N-oxide; the fermentation detoxifies. Documented food-safety pharmacology.
- *Funa-zushi* (Japanese fermented carp, Lake Biwa) — the ancient predecessor of modern sushi; fish + rice fermentation.
- *Fish miso* and other regional Japanese fermented-fish products.
- *Korean jeotgal* — fermented salted fish and shrimp pastes.

The fermentation transforms the fish *both pharmacologically* (free amino acids, peptides, biogenic amines, microbial metabolites) and *gastronomically* (umami, complex aromas, long-keeping). The classical saṃskāra reading is exactly right: *the substance is transformed by the process into a different substance with different properties.*

=== 7. Dried (sun, smoke, air)

- *Stockfish (Norwegian tørrfisk, air-dried unsalted cod)* — *air-dried by sub-Arctic wind*; reconstituted in water; foundational Norwegian and Italian (Venetian _stoccafisso_) tradition.
- *Bombay duck (bummalo, dried Harpadon)* — Indian.
- *Pirarucu seco* (Amazonian).

=== 8. Roe and viscera

- *Caviar* (Caspian sturgeon roe — the great wild populations now severely depleted; modern farmed Caspian sturgeon caviar replaces).
- *Ikura* (Japanese salmon roe).
- *Bottarga* (Mediterranean cured fish roe — Sardinian, Sicilian, Spanish).
- *Karasumi* (Japanese fish roe).
- *Fish liver oil*: *cod liver oil* (foundational vitamin A/D source in Northern European and modern Western medicine) and other fish-liver preparations; the *rasāyana* of the modern preventive-medicine tradition.

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

*Common to fish broadly:*
- *High-quality protein* — complete amino-acid profile; ~15–25 g per 100 g; among the best protein:digestibility ratios of any food.
- *Long-chain omega-3 (EPA, DHA)* — the principal modern reason for the cardiovascular-and-developmental fish recommendation; *2–4 g per 100 g in oily fish, much less in lean fish*. EPA and DHA are not interchangeable with the plant-derived ALA (α-linolenic acid); the conversion efficiency is poor (~5–10%); for these long-chain omega-3, *fish (or marine algae) is the principal dietary source*.
- *Vitamin D* — substantial in oily fish, especially cold-water; one of the few dietary sources.
- *Vitamin B12* — exclusive to animal-source foods; fish is a major source.
- *Vitamin A* — substantial in oily fish, especially liver oils.
- *Selenium* — modest to substantial.
- *Iodine* — particularly high in marine fish and seaweed.
- *Choline* — modest to substantial.

*Why fish is preventive-medicine attention:* the convergent _cardiovascular + neurodevelopmental + neurocognitive + anti-inflammatory_ pharmacology of long-chain omega-3 is among the strongest evidence bases for any single food category in clinical preventive medicine.

== Safety considerations

The principal practical and clinical safety axes for modern fish consumption:

=== Mercury (methylmercury bioaccumulation)

Mercury (specifically methylmercury, the organic, bioaccumulative form) is the *single most-cited modern fish-safety concern*. The principles:

- *Bioaccumulation* — mercury enters the marine food web at low levels and bioaccumulates up the trophic chain. Small short-lived fish accumulate little; large long-lived apex predators accumulate much.
- *Highest-mercury fish (limit or avoid in pregnancy and pediatric):* swordfish, shark, marlin, king mackerel, tilefish, large bluefin and bigeye tuna.
- *Mid-mercury fish:* yellowfin tuna, halibut, monkfish, sea bass, snapper, grouper, mahi-mahi.
- *Low-mercury fish (preferred for frequent consumption):* sardines, anchovies, herring, salmon (wild Pacific and farmed Atlantic), trout, sole, flounder, cod, pollock, tilapia, catfish.
- *Pregnancy:* current FDA / EPA guidance (verify against current regulatory text): 2–3 servings/week of low-mercury fish (e.g. sardines, salmon); avoid highest-mercury species.

=== PCBs, dioxins, and persistent organic pollutants

- Bioaccumulate in fish fat.
- Higher in fatty fish from contaminated waters (Great Lakes historically, certain estuaries).
- Higher in some farmed salmon historically (improving with regulation and feed sourcing).
- Liver is the typical accumulation site; *cod liver oil and similar are commercially purified to reduce PCBs*.

=== Microplastics

Documented in many marine fish; clinical significance evolving; smaller pelagic fish (sardines, anchovies) lower than coastal / benthic species.

=== Parasites

- *Anisakis* (and related nematodes) in raw / undercooked marine fish — causes anisakiasis; can cause severe allergic reaction and gastric symptoms. *Freezing at −20 °C for 7 days or −35 °C for 15 hours* is the standard mitigation for raw-fish preparations; cooking to 63 °C kills.
- *Diphyllobothrium* (broad fish tapeworm) in raw freshwater fish.
- Modern sushi-grade requirements include parasite-controlled sourcing and prior freezing.
- *Ceviche citrus marination does not reliably kill parasites* despite popular belief.

=== Antibiotic residues (farmed)

Aquaculture has historically used substantial antibiotics; modern certifications (ASC) and regulatory controls have reduced but not eliminated; reputable farmed-fish sourcing matters.

=== Algal biotoxins

- *Ciguatera* (ciguatoxin) — reef fish in tropical waters (barracuda, large grouper, snapper); heat-stable; can cause weeks-to-months of neurological symptoms; *do not eat large reef fish from known ciguatera zones*.
- *Scombroid (histamine) poisoning* — improperly-stored tunas, mackerel, mahi-mahi; histamine accumulation from bacterial degradation; not destroyed by cooking. Sourcing and refrigeration matter.
- *Tetrodotoxin* (fugu / puffer fish) — controlled by licensed-only preparation in Japan; not a general concern outside that tradition.
- *Domoic acid, saxitoxin* — shellfish-toxin concerns, less typically fish.

=== Allergy

Fish allergy is one of the major food allergies (especially in Mediterranean and Scandinavian populations); cross-reactivity within fish families is common.

=== Sustainability

Sustainable certification (MSC for wild, ASC for farmed; regional seafood-watch programs) provides practical guidance; many wild fisheries are stressed; aquaculture varies in environmental impact by species and method.

== Pregnancy and pediatric considerations

- *Pregnancy*: 2–3 servings/week of *low-mercury* oily fish (sardines, salmon, anchovies, trout) is *positively recommended* for fetal neurodevelopment (the omega-3 contribution); avoid *highest-mercury* fish.
- *Breastfeeding*: same principles.
- *Infants and toddlers*: oily-fish introduction at appropriate ages; avoid raw fish; small portions of low-mercury fish.
- *Allergy introduction*: current pediatric guidance favors early controlled introduction of fish around 6 months (to reduce allergy risk), in contrast to old advice to delay; verify against current pediatric guidance.

== Risk groups (general)

- *Pregnancy*: mercury and parasites (raw fish).
- *Severe immunocompromise*: avoid raw fish (parasites, bacterial).
- *Gout*: high-purine fish (sardines, anchovies, mackerel, herring) — limit.
- *Hyperuricemia*: similar.
- *Fish allergy*: avoid.
- *Anticoagulant therapy*: very high-dose fish-oil supplements (multiple grams of EPA/DHA daily) have mild antiplatelet effects; routine fish consumption is fine.
- *Kidney disease in late stages*: protein restriction may apply.

== Reference framing

- *Routine consumption*: 2–3 servings/week of low-mercury fish, with emphasis on oily fish, is the broadly-recommended general adult / pregnancy register.
- *Cured / smoked / fermented preparations*: are valuable cultural foods but typically higher in salt and modest in nitrate / nitrite (cured); moderate consumption.
- *Raw fish*: requires controlled sourcing and prior freezing for parasite safety; pregnant women and immunocompromised should typically avoid.
- *Wild vs. farmed*: both are nutritionally valuable; consider sourcing certifications.
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice; consult a clinician for specific contexts.*

